this is a production of cornell  university  so  i am chris smart i am a plant  pathologist i study diseases of plants  mostly vegetables but we also have  several projects on hemp  uh my student patrick and i talked this  morning about root rot diseases in hemp  and this afternoon uh my student ally  calla who just completed her ph.d  [Laughter]  she got her phd studying hemp powdery  mildew and ali is going to tell us  a bit about her work  uh including on the host range of the  hemp powdery mildew and also about the  impact of um fungicides and  diseases on cannabinoid content in hemp  so ally cool  hi everybody  good afternoon  so like chris was saying my dissertation  research has focused mostly on  powdery mildew on hemp  powdery mildew on hemp is caused by a  fungal pathogen called  gulab gulabinomyces ambrosia  and  if you know anything about powdery  mildews there are lots and lots of  different species of powdery mildews  that affect different crops and they  tend to be very host specific which just  means that  a single powdery mildew tends to only  infect a single host or closely related  hosts  so  but we have actually found something  contradictory to that in hemp powdery  mildew we first identified hemp powdery  mildew here in new york state in 2019  and that's when i began working on this  project and  was looking through the literature just  to find out about  um  more about this pathogen and see what  was already out there and i actually  found  there wasn't a lot of literature but we  did find that  this particular species was being  reported on a variety of different crops  so that was interesting and a little bit  confusing because like i said powdery  mildews tend to have a very specific  host range so i started doing some  growth chamber experiments in 2020  just taking powdery mildew that we had  gotten from hemp samples in the field  and using that powdery mildew to  inoculate a variety of different crops  so we inoculated  sunflower  a few cucurbits and okra to begin with  and we found that the powdery was able  to infect  um  some of the good carpets particularly  cucurbite pepo  the sunflower  and the okra  so chris is walking around with  some  leaf samples of some of those crops that  we're seeing this powdery mildew on  we also  did a field trial last year where we  expanded  the species that we  the  host species that we were screening so  we screened  a variety of cucurbita people  varieties  uh some a few different  sunflower cultivars okra cultivars  and zinnia cultivars all of which were  susceptible to this pottery mildew  species  but one thing we did find in the field  was that all the cucurbita  pipo species varieties that we screened  while we saw that they were susceptible  in the growth chamber experiments we all  we saw in the field  they became very infected and when we  um  sequenced those samples to identify them  we found a different  powdery mildew species on the cucurbits  so the species we found was phosphorous  anthei and this is another  pottery mildew species that is the main  causal agent of cucurbit powdery mildew  so what we think is happening there is  that the cucurbits are susceptible to  this hemp powdery mildew but the  pedostera species is out competing in  the field when they're both  present  um  so this was just something to maybe keep  in mind when you're out planting  being mindful of  other crops that you're planting nearby  or maybe in a  controlled environment like something  like this or greenhouse just being  mindful of  the crop species that you're planting  nearby  if you're trying to control for powdery  mildew  so the other project that chris  mentioned was our fungicide  efficacy trials um i i've done two  years of trials now um  last year i presented some data on that  showing we found a few  products that are labeled for hemp in  new york that were effective  at reducing powdery mildew disease  severity  and  then we repeated that trial last year so  now we have two years of data to show  that  those products were effective  one of one of them is the cell matrix  which is the potassium silicate  and i believe is also part of this trial  so savannah we'll talk a bit about that  in a bit and then the other product we  found that was  successful in reducing powdery mildew  was a  double nickel which is a bacillus  species of biocontrol  product  we also tested in those trials whether  both the pathogen infection so powdery  mildew infection  and  fungicide  treatment would we tested to see whether  those would affect cannabinoid  production so we tested these on high on  a high cannabinoid  in high  high cannabidiol and high  cbg  cultivars and we tested all of the main  cannabinoids  at the end of the season and we found  that  neither pathogen infection or  the fungicide treatment  impacted  cannabinoid production in both ears and  both different cultivars  so yeah do you want to add anything  chris  yeah so i will also say that  powdery mildew here at cornell uh  sorry powdery mildew is the pathogen  that we are furthest along in breeding  for resistance  um  ally actually uh did a screen  and did all the rating of a screen of a  large number of  cultivars of hemp  and we have identified uh several  different genes that are responsible for  susceptibility or resistance to powdery  mildew and hemp and we're working with  the breeding program to get those  incorporated into  the hemp cultivars that they release and  so we're really excited one of the genes  that we identified is actually  a susceptibility gene  so when that protein is in the plant the  plant is susceptible to powdery mildew  but in the cultivar that we identified  that was resistant it was really  completely resistant and that gene had a  natural mutation in it the plant grew  fine  so far we haven't seen any impacts on  yield but that natural mutation was  preventing powdery mildew from attacking  the plant so this would be optimal if we  could uh  use through the breeding program breathe  that into a larger number of uh  cultivars so we're really excited um  about about the powdery mildew work uh  not both the control the fungicide  control strategies as well as resistance  strategies and i think what it will take  uh to control powdery mildew is going to  be an integrated approach of resistant  cultivars  lots of airflow  like you see here  [Music]  along with  potentially some of the biofungicides  that ali found to be effective so  questions for ellie  no questions wow  do any of these products affect  testing  testing  so the question is do any of the  products affect testing um  so  that's something that  um is an area that we would like to look  more into specifically like  testing for microbials since they are  uh  bio control products so that's not  something we've looked at yet but it's  something that um  would be  in a future thing we want to look at  yeah a lot of growers will stop spraying  uh bacillus products two weeks prior to  harvest because uh you know  if the microbial testing that happens is  just a total number of colony forming  units that you might get back on a  quality report  bacillus will come up as a  colony-forming unit it will grow on the  media  that it's plated on so  as ali said that's one of the things we  want to work with the  you know the folks that are making the  regulations to make sure that everyone  knows exactly what microbials are being  tested for and um if bacillus is being  tested for then when do we actually need  to stop spraying that biological which  is controlling the pathogen to make sure  that you don't get red flagged in the in  the microbial testing uh  the products that we tested do not  increase um heavy metal or anything else  it would just be the microbials  [Applause]  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

